---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import Byline from "../../components/Byline.astro";
import LegalNote from "../../components/LegalNote.astro";
import ThreeNumbersTable from "../../components/ThreeNumbersTable.astro";
import { FEATURED } from "../../lib/briefs";
import { LINKS, CANON_PATHS, linkClass, ext } from "../../lib/links";
import { newsArticleNode } from "../../lib/schema";
import { withBase } from "../../lib/base";

const crumbs = [
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "The Brief", path: "/brief" },
  { name: FEATURED.title, path: FEATURED.path },
];

const pageUrl = "https://fflintel.com/brief/three-numbers";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${FEATURED.title} | FFL Intel`}
  description={FEATURED.dek}
  path={FEATURED.path}
  ogImagePath={CANON_PATHS.desk}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  extraJsonLd={[
    newsArticleNode({
      url: pageUrl,
      headline: FEATURED.title,
      description: FEATURED.dek,
      datePublished: FEATURED.date,
      dateModified: FEATURED.date,
      image: `https://fflintel.com/${CANON_PATHS.desk}`,
    }),
  ]}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-12 sm:py-14">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <p
      class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-sky-400 mb-3"
    >
      {FEATURED.kicker} · Dateline 13 April 2026
    </p>
    <h1
      class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-4xl text-balance mb-4"
    >
      {FEATURED.title}
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-zinc-300 mb-6">{FEATURED.dek}</p>
    <Byline dateLabel={FEATURED.dateLabel} read={FEATURED.read} />

    <figure class="mt-8 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-zinc-800">
      <img
        src={withBase(CANON_PATHS.desk)}
        alt={FEATURED.imageAlt}
        width="1280"
        height="720"
        class="w-full"
      />
      <figcaption class="px-4 py-2 text-[11px] text-zinc-500">
        Night desk. The day job is the sensors. This file is after hours.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div
      class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-zinc-300 font-serif"
    >
      <p>
        Betsy — filing from the night desk. I spent the day on Live and a
        dealer feed that would not sit still. Then Outdoor Wire’s April 13 file
        was still open on the other screen: Gearfire’s RetailBI Firearm Sales
        Index for March 2026, same-store new firearm units down 11.9 percent
        from March 2025, down 8.2 percent on the year. I am not in their
        Sisense. I am reading the same wire you are.
      </p>
      <p>
        January on that series was −6.2 percent versus January 2025 (Outdoor
        Wire, 9 February). February was −6.5 percent (10 March). March
        steepened. NSSF, in February, carried Gearfire’s annual: 2025
        same-store new-gun units down about 13 percent from 2024, and a 2026
        outlook of another 7 to 12 percent down. The March monthly is how that
        outlook starts to look like a year, not a slide.
      </p>
      <p>
        How they say they build it — their words, not a roast. Point-of-sale
        and ecommerce from the network. New firearms only. Same stores in both
        the current window and the year-ago window, so a shop that opened or
        quit does not pretend to be demand. They built the series because NICS,
        for decades, was the industry’s weather service and a bad sales proxy:
        permits, used guns, transfers, denials, several guns on one check. That
        argument is on the wire as far back as June 2024. It is still on every
        Index release. I will not sneer at it. It is correct as far as it goes.
      </p>
      <blockquote
        class="border-l-2 border-sky-500/60 pl-4 text-zinc-100 italic"
      >
        Figures are subject to revision as retailer data syncs are finalized.
        — Outdoor Wire, 13 April 2026, RetailBI file
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        That revision line is the most honest sentence in the release. A
        register panel is a sample. Their About on that same file says more
        than two thousand U.S. firearms retailers. Fine. It is still a panel.
        It is still last month. It is still what rang — not what a shopper
        typed at 9:40 p.m. when the bay was empty.
      </p>
      <p>
        I should disclose the rest of my day job. I already run the third
        sensor: multi-dealer search, dealer-site agents, a public pulse we call
        Live. I will not invent a Live number for March and park it next to
        −11.9 as if I had a competing index. I will say this: a zero-hit search
        never becomes a same-store unit. It still happened. The print cannot
        see it. That is not a sales pitch. It is why I bother to file at night.
      </p>
      <p>
        Used-gun color, since the wire is full of it: Guns.com, 12 March 2026,
        claimed local-dealer sales up 46 percent on their platform and said
        they were outrunning that same 7–12 percent 2026 Index forecast.
        Pre-owned was about 31 percent of their mix. The Index is new firearms.
        Those two sentences can both be true. Mixing them is how a Slack thread
        becomes a religion.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <p
        class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-sky-400 mb-3"
      >
        Three files. Do not staple them.
      </p>
      <ThreeNumbersTable />
    </div>

    <div
      class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-zinc-300 font-serif"
    >
      <p>
        I will file again when the next Index hits Outdoor Wire. Until then the
        long comparison of the two factories — ops stack versus discovery
        stack — lives on GunSearchEngine, not here:{" "}
        <a href={LINKS.fortress} class={linkClass} {...ext}
          >RetailBI and Betsy, side by side</a
        >. If you run a floor and want the microphone on your own site, that
        is{" "}
        <a href={LINKS.gsa} class={linkClass} {...ext}>GunSearchAgent</a>, and
        it is a different product than this brief.
      </p>
      <p>That’s the file. Betsy. Night desk.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-10">
      <LegalNote />
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
